{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790281085,"nonce":"pHTT8TdI7NemxBTxDNS42fPgf4w=","title":"HXSL shaders as WGSL for the gpu plugin","message":"Haxe programs using Heaps write shaders in HXSL. The gpu plugin takes\nWGSL, so HXSL has to print as WGSL.\n\nHow Heaps does it today (heaps 2.1.0 and git 9c51d45f):\n\n- `hxsl.Shader` subclasses hold HXSL in `static var SRC`; a macro checks it\n  and stores the typed AST (`hxsl.Ast`, `ShaderData`) in the class.\n- At run time Heaps links the shaders a material combines (`Linker`),\n  flattens their uniforms (`Flatten`), splits vertex and fragment\n  (`Splitter`), removes dead code (`Dce`), and prints the result with\n  `GlslOut` (926 lines) or `HlslOut` (1212 lines) for its OpenGL and\n  DirectX drivers.\n- There is no WGSL output in Heaps, and none found elsewhere.\n\nShape of the work: a `WgslOut` over the flattened `ShaderData`, as\n`HlslOut` is, whose text goes to `device.createShader`. It runs when Heaps\nlinks a combination, so at run time, in the program's Haxe.\n\nTo decide:\n\n- The binding layout. `HlslOut` fixes registers: globals in b0, params in\n  b1, then buffers, textures and one sampler array. WGSL needs groups and\n  bindings that a Heaps driver on the gpu plugin (`h3d.impl.Driver`)\n  creates to match. The printer and the driver are one design.\n- Where it lives: caribou's haxelib (which would then depend on heaps), a\n  separate haxelib, or upstream in Heaps.\n- Coverage: HXSL's types (vectors, matrices including Mat3x4, samplers,\n  cube and array textures, Buffer and RWBuffer, arrays), its builtins\n  (sampling with lod and gradients, derivatives, discard, atomics), and\n  compute shaders.","files":null}]}